    "description": "Iranian technology media bring together technology news, product reviews, practical instruction, and reporting about the domestic digital economy. Digiato and Zoomit illustrate this combination, although their own descriptions cannot establish their relative reach or editorial independence.",
    "tags": ["Technology journalism", "Persian media", "Product reviews", "Digital economy", "Media literacy"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:14:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:14:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Technology Media in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iranian technology journalism through Digiato and Zoomit, including product reviews, practical guides, domestic reporting, and evidence limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More than technology headlines",
        "content": "Iranian technology publishing is useful to understand as a mixture of journalism and service information. Digiato and Zoomit both organize material around news, reviews, instruction, and buying decisions. Their editorial menus therefore address different questions: what has happened, how a device performs, how software works, and what a reader might consider before purchasing equipment. These formats share a subject but require different kinds of evidence. A product announcement establishes what a manufacturer claims; a review should establish what the reviewer actually examined. [s1][s3]\n\nThese publications also connect international technology with an Iranian readership. Digiato identifies Iranian technology as a dedicated subject alongside hardware, software, networks, and security. Zoomit's institutional history describes the establishment of a domestic technology reporting team. This distinction matters: reporting on a global product release and reporting on the institutions or businesses shaping technology inside Iran are separate editorial tasks. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "publishers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Two examples and their development",
        "content": "Digiato's introduction dates its beginning to Ordibehesht 1393 in the Iranian Solar Hijri calendar. It describes a group of experienced writers coming together from earlier Persian websites and presents multimedia work as part of its intended approach. Its statements about professionalism and freedom from commercialization are editorial aspirations expressed by the publisher; they are not an independent assessment of its output. [s1]\n\nZoomit dates its beginnings to Solar Hijri year 1389. Its own chronology describes a progression from technology news and buying guidance into specialist reviews, original video, domestic reporting, and a dedicated science team. It also identifies a publishing licence associated with the Zarehbin news outlet and the institution Qalam Ayandeh Iranian. These details establish how the outlet describes its development and legal identity, rather than demonstrating the quality of every article. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading across editorial formats",
        "content": "Zoomit's inspected website separates buying guides, reviews, tutorials, science, video, and Iranian coverage. It also presents Zoubin as a service providing product specifications and seller listings. The coexistence of editorial and shopping functions makes the purpose of an individual page important: specifications, seller information, laboratory findings, and purchase recommendations should not be treated as interchangeable evidence. [s3]\n\nFor international readers, the most useful approach is to identify the article's format before using its conclusions. Look for the original announcement behind news, an explicit testing method behind a review, and a publication or update date behind practical guidance. Neither a publisher's claim to popularity nor an undated traffic counter establishes a comparable audience ranking. The sources inspected here support a concrete account of these outlets' stated activities, but not a league table of Iranian technology journalism. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
